+# Touch Map and Minimal-Diff Strategy for the herdr mosh-like fork
+
+Scope: `github.com/alepar/herdr` (remote `fork`), tracking `herdrdev/herdr` (`origin`, about 1596 commits in 6 months). Citations are relative to `/var/home/alepar/AleCode/herdr` at `5608de06`. **[re-verified]** marks points that the workstreams or critics disputed and that were checked against the source for this final version.
+
+---
+
+## 0. The decision this document turns on (D1)
+
+The workstreams put Phase 1 in two incompatible places.
+
+| | **A. Protocol-level Phase 1** (workstreams p1-acks, p1-input, p1-resume, p1-remote) | **B. Link-level Phase 1** (Eternal-Terminal style) |
+|---|---|---|
+| Where the resume logic lives | Inside the herdr endpoint protocol: surface-ack ring, sequenced input envelope, server-side detached-client record, `--remote` reconnect | In a resumable byte-stream session between a fork local bridge and a fork remote "keeper" process. The keeper holds the server socket open while ssh is down |
+| What the server and TUI see on an ssh kill | A disconnect, then a reconnect and a resume handshake | No disconnect. The server's `ClientConnection` stays registered and the client socket stays open. The client sees a pause, kept alive by injected keepalives (§2.1) |
+| Lines in existing files | about 220 | **about 8** |
+| Hot files touched | headless.rs about 13, client/mod.rs about 15, client_transport.rs about 25, render_stream.rs 14, handshake.rs 12, supervisor.rs 12, surface_reuse.rs 40 | none: main.rs, headless.rs, client/*, render_stream.rs, client_transport.rs, handshake.rs, wire.rs, endpoint.rs and app/mod.rs are all untouched |
+| Exactly-once delivery | Pane and popup input only. EndpointRequests are excluded (p1-input risk 5) | Every client-to-server byte, including EndpointRequests, resize and focus. Delivery is exactly once, but the *semantics* are not (see the row below) |
+| Late-delivery semantics | Same issue for the covered subset | Client-side deadlines fire during an outage and the buffered request still runs later. `ENDPOINT_COMMAND_TIMEOUT` = 60 s (`client/endpoint_commands.rs:12`) **[re-verified]**: the command is reported failed and then executes on resume, so a user retry runs it twice. `ACTIVATION_TIMEOUT` = 5 s (`client/endpoint/activation.rs:14`) **[re-verified]**: surface on/off pairs replay after the client's rollback (`client/mod.rs:2304-2323`). Buffered clicks and navigation keys replay against a layout another client may have changed |
+| No full snapshot on resume | Only when the client decoder baseline identity matches the server's parked baseline. That needs the ack ring (p1-resume risk 2) | Always within the grace period, because nothing was torn down. A deferred render is a pending flag, not a forced repaint (`clients.rs:453-465`) |
+| Server-side state while a client is down | The detached record is not an active client | The parked connection is still an active client. That has real effects (geometry, foreground state, focus, held keys). See §4.4 |
+| Survives server restart or live handoff | No (new `boot_id` gives a fresh session) | No. The keeper forwards EOF cut at a frame boundary and today's reconnect runs (§4.4) |
+| Reused by Phase 3 | The ack ring, resync and relaxed chain are prerequisites for QUIC drop-on-supersede | The keeper becomes the Phase 3 QUIC daemon, and the session layer becomes the QUIC stream's resume layer. Drop-on-supersede still needs the A-style ack ring, which moves to Phase 3b |
+
+**Recommendation: B for Phase 1, and A's surface-ack and resync work moved into Phase 3b.**
+
+Why this holds up:
+- Over SSH the stream is reliable, so frames are never lost except at disconnect. B makes disconnects lossless.
+- p1-acks reached the same conclusion: under the Optimistic (newest-sent) base on ordered transports, acks do no work in Phase 1 except feed resume.
+- B meets the Phase 1 acceptance criteria (ssh kill or netem loss leads to resume, zero lost input, no full snapshot) with a few percent of A's diff, and without touching any hot region.
+
+This departs from the plan's Phase 1 bullet list (`mosh-like-herdr-plan.md:5-13`), which names the protocol mechanisms explicitly, so it needs a decision. Section 2.2 keeps A in condensed form as the fallback design and as the Phase 3b work list.
+
+Facts B depends on, all re-verified:
+- Shell clients have no server-side write timeout. Only observers get `OBSERVER_WRITE_TIMEOUT` = 30 s (`server/client_transport.rs:45, 1076-1079`). A keeper that stops reading stalls the server writer; it does not disconnect it.
+- The render lane is a single slot, latest-wins (`client_transport.rs:320-330`) **[re-verified]**. The control lane is a plain `VecDeque` with **no cap** (`send_control`, `client_transport.rs:310-318`) **[re-verified]**. It carries notifications, snapshots, agent completions and Kitty `Graphics` payloads. A keeper that stops reading for a long time therefore grows server memory. §2.1 1b chooses the keeper's drain policy to avoid that.
+- The client counts any inbound `ServerMessage` as liveness before dispatch (`client/mod.rs:1457-1458`). An unknown `EndpointControl` kind decodes to `Ignored`; only `shell.snapshot.*` kinds are fatal (`client/endpoint/control.rs:61-66`, handled at `client/mod.rs:2139-2142`). The local bridge can therefore inject link frames harmlessly, under the rules in §2.1.
+- Saved-machine health is a 5 s idle ping with a 10 s timeout (`client/endpoint/health.rs:3-4`). Without injected keepalives, a resume must finish within about 10 s of the last ping, or the client tears down and does a full reconnect.
+- `SshStdioBridge::start_command` (`remote/attach.rs:3030`, `pub(super)`) spawns one ssh per accepted local connection, strips the preamble and supervises the child (`attach.rs:3259-3394`). `bridge_child(stream, command, stop)` (`attach.rs:3287`) takes a caller-built `Command`. A descendant module of `attach` can drive either one per leg without editing their bodies.
+- `SshStdioBridge` is `{local_socket, socket_identity, should_stop, failure_rx, thread}` (`attach.rs:2996-3002`) **[re-verified]**. Its `Drop` (`attach.rs:3108-3120`) sets `should_stop`, removes the socket if owned and joins the thread. A descendant module can build one with a struct literal around the local-bridge thread, so no wrapper enum is needed (§2.1 1c).
