+## 1. Diff-minimization principles
+
+### 1.1 Rules
+
+1. **Put fork code in a fork tree.** All new code goes under `src/mosh/`, plus `tests/mosh_*.rs`, `scripts/mosh-diff-budget.sh` and, in Phase 3, possibly `crates/herdr-mosh-net/`. `git diff upstream/master -- ':!src/mosh' ':!tests/mosh_*' ':!scripts/mosh-*' ':!HERDR_MOSH_VISION.md'` is the whole review surface. `scripts/mosh-diff-budget.sh` fails if that diff grows past a budget (20 lines through Phase 2). It is run by hand at each upstream merge, not as a `.github/workflows` job, because the workflow tree is upstream-owned and a fork job there is itself a conflict surface.
+
+2. **Mount the fork tree with `#[path]`, not visibility bumps or a `main.rs` line.**
+   - The `mod` block in `main.rs` (l.14-65) had 28 commits in 6 months **[re-verified]**, so it is not a quiet anchor.
+   - Phase 1 mounts the whole tree with **one line in `src/remote/attach.rs`**: `#[path = "../mosh/mod.rs"] pub(super) mod mosh; // mosh-fork hook`. A `#[path]` in a non-mod.rs file resolves relative to that file's directory, so this means `src/mosh/mod.rs`. Because the target file is named `mod.rs`, its own `mod session;` lines resolve under `src/mosh/`.
+   - Every fork module is then a descendant of `remote::attach`. It can reach private items: `RemoteHerdr` fields (`attach.rs:475-481`), `RemoteExecutable::{command, session_args}` (`attach.rs:367-394`), `ManagedSshOptions` fields (`attach.rs:725-731`), `SshStdioBridge` fields, `remote_client_status` (`attach.rs:2019`), `remote_binary_candidates`, `posix_remote_output_command` and `bridge_child`.
+   - Visibility: `pub(super) mod mosh` makes the module visible across `remote`. Items that `remote::saved` and `remote::host` call (`start_ui_bridge`, `remote::dispatch`) must be declared `pub(in crate::remote)`; `pub(super)` inside a submodule of `mosh` would reach only `mosh`.
+   - Later phases that need `impl HeadlessServer` or client internals mount *separate* fork files under those owners, for example `#[path = "../mosh/server_hooks.rs"] mod mosh_hooks;` next to the `mod` list at `headless.rs:77-85`. A file can be mounted only once, so each later module belongs to exactly one owner. If Phase 4 client code needs to call into the shared session code, that is when `mod mosh;` moves to `main.rs` (1 line), not before.
+   - Upstream already uses `#[path]` this way (`server/headless/tests/mod.rs:5-13`). If upstream renames an owner file, the build fails loudly and the fix is one line.
+
+3. **Hooks are one statement at a stable anchor, tagged.** Every edit to an upstream file is a single call, match arm, field or predicate, ending in `// mosh-fork hook`. After each upstream merge, `git grep -n 'mosh-fork'` lists the whole touch surface.
+
+4. **Prefer swap points to edits.** The two `SshStdioBridge::start(` UI call sites are the Phase 1 and Phase 3 transport seams: `attach.rs:90` in `run_remote` (4 commits in 6 months) and `saved.rs:35` in `connect_saved_ssh` (l.30-46: 3 commits).
+   - The third caller, `probe_remote_endpoint` (`attach.rs:2388-2405`), is **intentionally left plain**. It is metadata-only and uses the same `local_forward_socket_path` as `run_remote` (`attach.rs:57`, `2392`). The local bridge's `prepare_socket_path` relies on the probe having finished first. The test callers at `attach.rs:4012` and `4115` are unaffected.
+   - Hooking the first statement of `SshStdioBridge::start` (l.3005, 6 commits) instead of both call sites was considered and rejected. `start` does not receive the `RemoteSsh`, so the capability probe would have to rebuild one from private `ManagedSshConfig` layout.
+   - The remote entry is a guard arm in `remote/host.rs:7-21`. That avoids the `main.rs:565-567` dispatch and the `main.rs:767` allow-list. The `remote-client-bridge` dispatch runs before the allow-list **[re-verified]**.
+
+5. **Reuse upstream internals by calling them, never by copying them.** `start_command`, `bridge_child`, `posix_remote_output_command`, `RemoteExecutable::session_args`, `remote_binary_candidates` and `remote_client_status` are all reachable from the `#[path]` tree. The attach.rs bridge internals had 9 commits in the last 30 days, and copies would drift silently.
+
+6. **Leave frozen contracts byte-identical:**
+   - `ClientMessage` and `ServerMessage` variant order, `PROTOCOL_VERSION` = 22 and `ENDPOINT_PROTOCOL_GENERATION` = 1;
+   - `EndpointClientHello` fields (`protocol/endpoint.rs:46-75`), the bincode structs `PaneSurfacePatch` and `PaneSurfaceFrame`;
+   - `tests/fixtures/endpoint-method-shapes-v1.json`.
+
+7. **Prefix every fork-introduced name** with `x-mosh` / `x-mosh.`: EndpointControl kinds, status capability strings, bridge flags and env vars. An upstream name added later then cannot collide with different semantics. The workstream names such as `endpoint.surface-ack.v1` become `x-mosh.surface-ack.v1`, unless and until the piece is upstreamed under upstream's name.
+
+8. **Do not add hello fields.** Each new `EndpointClientHello` bool spreads to `ServerEvent::ClientShellConnected { .. }` literals: 16 in tests (`headless/tests/mod.rs` 11, `tests/surface_interest.rs` 5) and 8 in source (`client_transport.rs` 4, `headless.rs` 4). If a later phase must opt in per connection:
+   - the client merges an `x_mosh` object into the serialized hello JSON (there is no `deny_unknown_fields` on the struct, `endpoint.rs:46`);
+   - the server parses `data` a second time into a fork struct;
+   - better still, opt in implicitly by sending the first fork control message, as p1-acks does.
+
+9. **Use runtime capability negotiation, not cargo features.** Upstream has no `[features]` table. Remote capability detection reuses the open `endpoint_capabilities` list in `status client --json` (`cli/status.rs:298-302`), parsed into `RemoteClientStatusJson.endpoint_capabilities` (`attach.rs:2420`).
+   - The fork tree is not reachable from `cli` (rule 2), so `status.rs` pushes the **string literal** `"x-mosh-resume-bridge-v1"`. `src/mosh/mod.rs` holds the same constant, and a unit test asserts that `status client --json` output contains it.
+   - The kill switch is an env var on the client side, read inside `src/mosh/` (`HERDR_MOSH_RESUME=0`). Phase 1 needs no config key.
+
+10. **Dependencies, lock file and identity:**
+    - Phase 1 adds no dependencies. Tokens come from `/dev/urandom`, hashed with the existing `sha2`.
+    - Phase 3 dependencies go in one comment-headed block, or in a workspace crate.
+    - Never hand-merge `Cargo.lock`. Take upstream's version with `git checkout --theirs Cargo.lock`, then run `cargo check`. Never run `cargo update` in the fork.
+    - Never edit `Cargo.toml` `version =`. Label fork builds with `HERDR_BUILD_CHANNEL` / `HERDR_BUILD_ID` (`build_info.rs:6,10`).
+
+11. **Tests live in fork files, and the crate is binary-only.** There is no `src/lib.rs` and no `[lib]` target **[re-verified]**. So:
+    - `tests/mosh_resume.rs` and `tests/mosh_multi_client.rs` can only drive the `herdr` binary as a subprocess, reusing `tests/support`. The integration precedent for fake ssh on PATH is `tests/remote_attach.rs:69-107`; `tests/live_handoff.rs` is the precedent for the handoff test.
+    - In-process tests of legs, `start_command` and `bridge_child` must be `#[cfg(test)]` modules inside `src/mosh/ssh_leg.rs`, which is a descendant of `attach`. The unit test at `attach.rs:3684-3709` shows the in-process fake-ssh technique.
+    - Never add cases to `headless/tests/mod.rs` or the `client_transport.rs` test module.
+
+12. **Merge upstream weekly.** At 9 commits per 30 days in headless.rs and 13 in client/mod.rs, one-line hooks resolve trivially only while the context is fresh.
+
+13. **Fix repo hygiene before the first fork commit.** The working tree has 22 mode-only changes (755→644, 0 insertions or deletions), including `.githooks/commit-msg` and `.githooks/pre-commit`. The hooks are therefore not executable, and the conventional-commit check is silently skipped. Run `git checkout -- <paths>` to restore the +x bits, and do not commit them. `core.fileMode=true`.
+
