+## 2. Per-phase touch tables
+
+### 2.1 Phase 1, recommended design B: resumable link session over SSH
+
+**How it fits together:**
+
+```
+herdr client --local sock--> local_bridge (fork) ==session==> [leg: internal sock -> ssh (start_command or bridge_child)
+  -> sshd -> `herdr remote-client-bridge --x-mosh-session-v1` (attacher)] --unix--> keeper (detached)
+  --unix--> herdr server client socket
+```
+
+**Lifecycle:**
+- On a leg failure, `local_bridge` keeps the client socket open and opens a new leg (fresh, BatchMode, one at a time; see 1d).
+- The session handshake exchanges `(token, recv_offset, grace, build_id)`, and both ends retransmit from the peer's offset.
+- The local bridge forwards **only whole frames** to the client. It holds a partial downstream frame until it completes, so an outage that starts mid-frame never leaves the client stream mid-frame, and injection is always possible.
+- Keepalive injection rules:
+  - only after the first complete downstream frame (the `Welcome`) has passed through, so `do_handshake` (`client/handshake.rs:165-310`, 60 s remote timeout at l.32) is never disturbed;
+  - only between whole frames;
+  - only while a resume attempt is active, and never past the local give-up deadline.
+- **Session end** is explicit and has four triggers. The keeper closes its server socket and drops the token on any of them:
+  1. Local client EOF. The local bridge sends `end` to the keeper, best effort over the live leg, and otherwise on the next leg it opens. For `--remote` the client is a child process (`attach.rs:3577-3599`) and the bridge lives in the parent. For saved machines the bridge is dropped with the transport (`supervisor.rs:476-484`).
+  2. Bridge drop or stop, including the shared `cancelled` flag in `connect_saved_ssh` (`saved.rs:24-43`). `SshStdioBridge::drop` joins the thread on the caller, so the local-bridge thread must poll `should_stop` at a short interval and never block on the network.
+  3. The grace period expires on the keeper. The local bridge has its own give-up deadline equal to the grace, because it cannot learn of the expiry while the host is unreachable. At the deadline it closes the client socket, and today's reconnect or exit path runs.
+  4. Ghost eviction. When the client gives up and does a full reconnect, the new session hello names the previous token from the same local bridge slot (saved profile id or `--remote` socket path), and the keeper evicts it at once. Without this the server sees two `ClientConnection`s for one physical client for the whole grace (§4.4).
